WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.920
Do you believe in the promise proclaimed&nbsp;
in the Declaration of Independence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.320
Do you believe that all of us are created&nbsp;
equal and that we have a natural right to life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.840
liberty, and the pursuit of happiness?&nbsp;
And do you also believe that we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.880
guarantee those rights to all Americans?
Of course, you do. So, let’s think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.480
what that means.
Let’s look back….

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.840
On January 11, 1944, President Franklin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.120
D. Roosevelt – FDR – delivered his 11th&nbsp;
State of the Union Message to Congress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.920
The United States was in the middle of&nbsp;
its biggest and most consequential war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.360
pitted against Nazi Germany and Imperial Japan.&nbsp;
But Roosevelt didn’t just talk about winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.280
the war. He also spoke of what Americans&nbsp;
needed to do to win the peace to come.  
&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.440
In his speech to Congress that day, he called for&nbsp;
a new Bill of Rights – an Economic Bill of Rights.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.240
In the 1930s, Roosevelt and the American&nbsp;
people had fought the Great Depression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.640
the worst economic and social catastrophe in US&nbsp;
history. Rallying to the President’s New Deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.080
they not only had revived the economy. They had&nbsp;
also subjected business and finance to public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.360
supervision and regulation, empowered the federal&nbsp;
government to address the needs of working people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.200
mobilized and organized labor&nbsp;
unions and civil rights groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.040
established a Social Security system, expanded&nbsp;
and upgraded the nation’s public infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.360
improved the environment, and&nbsp;
cultivated and promoted the arts.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.560
In 1941, however, Americans confronted a new&nbsp;
crisis – the Second World War. But here too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.600
they went all “All Out!” In fact, they not only&nbsp;
did all they could to fight Fascism overseas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.400
but also fought for democracy at home&nbsp;
by dramatically expanding the labor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.920
civil rights movements. And by early 1944,&nbsp;
there was good cause to believe both that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.880
Victory might soon be at hand and that&nbsp;
further progressive action was possible.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.840
At the outset of the State of the Union speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.640
Roosevelt urged Americans to sustain the&nbsp;
war effort. But he also now looked ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.720
confident that Americans – who had achieved so&nbsp;
much – wanted to not only revive the New Deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:16.240
but in every way expand upon it. Opinion polls&nbsp;
conducted in 1943 indicated, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:23.600
that 83 percent of Americans wanted a guarantee&nbsp;
of health care for all, 73 percent supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:30.880
launching new public works programs, and&nbsp;
79 percent wanted a federal jobs guarantee.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.880
Though he was too sick to appear in person&nbsp;
before Congress to deliver the speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.640
Roosevelt went on radio and delivered a spirited&nbsp;
address. And after reviewing the continuing war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.440
effort, he turned to the question of the&nbsp;
postwar peace effort in the United States.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.800
“This Republic,” he said, “had its beginning,&nbsp;
and grew to its present strength, under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.880
protection of certain inalienable rights.… They&nbsp;
were our rights to life and liberty. As our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.560
Nation has grown in size and stature, however&nbsp;
– as our industrial economy expanded – these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.000
political rights proved inadequate to assure&nbsp;
us equality in the pursuit of happiness.”

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.080
The words that followed are among the most&nbsp;
radical in Presidential history: “We have come”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:22.480
FDR contended, “to a clear realization of the fact&nbsp;
that true individual freedom cannot exist without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.680
economic security and independence.&nbsp;
‘Necessitous men are not free men.’”

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.520
What he then proposed would&nbsp;
be seen as far-left today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.560
Though, as he reminded his fellow Americans, it&nbsp;
was not a repudiation of the promises enshrined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.400
in the Declaration and Bill of Rights, but&nbsp;
a continuation and realization of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.080
Indeed, only with ECONOMIC rights&nbsp;
could POLITICAL rights be made real.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.000
As Roosevelt said:

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.080
In our day certain economic&nbsp;
truths have become accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.840
as self-evident. A Second Bill of Rights under which a new basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:09.242
of security and prosperity can be established&nbsp;
for all regardless of station, or race, or creed.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.600
The rights Roosevelt was proposing – a right&nbsp;
to a home, to healthcare, to earn enough money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.960
to live comfortably, a guaranteed job – would&nbsp;
be called “socialism” or even “communism” by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.080
the conservatives of today. But whatever&nbsp;
they might be labeled, they were rooted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:33.760
as FDR made clear, in America’s promise of&nbsp;
life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.960
Empowered by the aspirations of those who had&nbsp;
fought the Depression and were now fighting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.920
Fascism, Roosevelt was projecting a path to a&nbsp;
better, brighter, happier, and healthier future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.640
“All of these rights,” he said, “spell SECURITY.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.720
And after this war is won, we&nbsp;
must be prepared to move forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:59.840
in the implementation of these rights, to&nbsp;
new goals of human happiness and well-being.”

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.720
But FDR knew all too well that there&nbsp;
were those who would fiercely oppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:10.080
them – as they always had. And he warned&nbsp;
his fellow citizens against what he called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.120
“the grave dangers of rightist reaction.”

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.160
What Roosevelt laid out in his State&nbsp;
of the Union was something simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.480
but radical. It was that history wasn’t&nbsp;
something to be left in the past, but to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:31.440
constantly renewed and remade.&nbsp;
New times demand new freedoms.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.400
Just a few years earlier, his&nbsp;
Solicitor-General Robert H.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.680
Jackson – a future Supreme Court Justice –&nbsp;
told the members of the National Lawyers Guild:

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:47.280
“We too are Founders. We too are makers of&nbsp;
a nation. We too are called upon to write,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.040
to defend, and to make live, new bills of right.”

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.800
And this was a task that a majority of Americans&nbsp;
were ready to take up. At a demonstration in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:06:00.400
York City, 1.4 million people showed up to hear&nbsp;
Senator Robert Wagner enthusiastically defend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.400
the call for a Second Bill of&nbsp;
Rights. Labor and civil rights groups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.120
actively campaigned for it. And in the&nbsp;
presidential election later that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:16.480
Roosevelt won a fourth term as President&nbsp;
with a resounding 432 electoral votes.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.840
Roosevelt would not live to achieve his dream.&nbsp;
At his fourth and final inauguration in 1945,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.200
he appeared sick and frail. While he was getting&nbsp;
his portrait painted a few months later in Warm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.840
Springs, Georgia, he put his hand over&nbsp;
his forehead, slumped over, and died.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.400
The Second Bill of Rights was never realized. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.200
forces of “rightist reaction” that&nbsp;
FDR had warned of were too powerful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.680
Corporate executives and conservatives soon&nbsp;
took to fomenting Cold War fears and purging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.680
public life of leftists not only to block the&nbsp;
hoped-for revival and expansion of the New Deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:02.640
but also to crush the very idea and memory of&nbsp;
Roosevelt’s proposed Economic Bill of Rights.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.840
That doesn’t mean, of course, that his&nbsp;
vision has to remain shrouded and forgotten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:11.280
What FDR promised, though still radical, remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.840
deeply possible – if we have the&nbsp;
will to recover it and to advance it.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:24.480
We, too, can be Founders. We, too,&nbsp;
can be makers of a nation. We, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:30.640
are called upon to write, to defend,&nbsp;
to make live, new bills of rights.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.440
I’m Harvey Kaye, professor emeritus&nbsp;
of Democracy and Justice Studies at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:43.840
the University of Wisconsin-Green&nbsp;
Bay, for the Gravel Institute.

